WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.024 --> 00:00:09.189
In April of 1947, a baseball manager told his all-white team to insult the next man coming up to bat

2
00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:13.959
for the opposing team. The white players screamed hate-filled curses at the black man who approached. They called him names

3
00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:19.301
and shouted that his teammates would catch a disease if they touched him. That man, Jackie Robinson, was near despair, but

4
00:00:19.302 --> 00:00:25.843
he faced the hatred silently. He had to convince the world he was an excellent ballplayer and a true gentleman.

5
00:00:25.844 --> 00:00:31.391
He had to have the guts not to fight back. Robinson wondered whether he would ever be judged by his

6
00:00:31.392 --> 00:00:41.212
skills and not by the color of his skin. Although President Lincoln had freed the slaves about eighty years earlier,

7
00:00:41.213 --> 00:00:49.936
African Americans still did not have the same rights as white citizens. The country was segregated; blacks and whites usually

8
00:00:49.937 --> 00:00:57.484
did not mix in everyday life, including in sports. Robinson started a civil-rights movement that changed the world forever when

9
00:00:57.485 --> 00:01:06.653
he became the first black man to join a twentieth-century major-league team. Over his years on the team and through his

10
00:01:06.654 --> 00:01:15.798
work for civil rights, Robinson opened the door for other African Americans to claim their civil rights too. Jack Roosevelt

11
00:01:15.799 --> 00:01:28.059
"Jackie" Robinson was born on January 31, 1919, in Cairo, Georgia. The grandson of slaves, he was one of five

12
00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:39.443
children. His father, Jerry Robinson, a farmer on a plantation, deserted his family. Jackie’s mother, Mallie, displayed the bravery Jackie

13
00:01:39.444 --> 00:01:48.165
would need in years to come. In search of a better life for her family, she took the children and

14
00:01:48.166 --> 00:01:54.928
moved to California in 1920, when Jackie was 16 months old. She bought a house in a white neighborhood of

15
00:01:54.929 --> 00:02:00.646
Pasadena, even though the family was not welcome in the community. Because of their skin color, her children could swim in

16
00:02:00.647 --> 00:02:05.994
the town pool only once a week. They weren’t welcome at the local youth center either and occasionally fought with

17
00:02:05.995 --> 00:02:12.922
whites. Jackie’s mother had trouble putting enough food on the table for her energetic family, but Jackie found a way

18
00:02:12.923 --> 00:02:21.804
to help out. Like his siblings, he loved sports and was an excellent player, so other kids offered to share

19
00:02:21.805 --> 00:02:26.072
their lunches with him if he played on their teams. "You might say I turned professional at an early age,"

20
00:02:26.073 --> 00:02:37.748
he later wrote. The Robinsons were thrilled when Jackie’s older brother Mack won the silver Olympic medal in Berlin for the

21
00:02:37.749 --> 00:02:49.951
two-hundred-meter run. Jackie, too, soon became a star athlete. Robinson won a sports scholarship to the University of California at

22
00:02:49.952 --> 00:02:54.385
Los Angeles and attended the university for two years, excelling in football, basketball, track, and baseball. Nevertheless, he dropped out

23
00:02:54.386 --> 00:03:01.561
to help his mother take care of the family. He worked as an athletic director with underprivileged kids until the

24
00:03:01.562 --> 00:03:09.666
U.S. entered World War II, when he joined the army. But after three years of service, he returned to sports

25
00:03:09.667 --> 00:03:21.328
to make his living. He spent one year playing in baseball’s Negro leagues. Then, in August 1945, the talented 26-year-old

26
00:03:21.329 --> 00:03:29.750
ballplayer agreed to meet with Branch Rickey, the president and general manager of the Brooklyn Dodgers, an all-white baseball team. Rickey

27
00:03:29.751 --> 00:03:38.630
wanted to integrate the all-white major leagues, and he was looking for the right man to start what he called

28
00:03:38.631 --> 00:03:46.020
his "noble experiment." He warned Robinson about the danger he would face; balls would be thrown directly at him, and

29
00:03:46.021 --> 00:03:55.673
he would receive hate mail and death threats. Still, Robinson promised that for the next three years, he would remain

30
00:03:55.674 --> 00:04:00.561
silent throughout any abuse he might suffer. He would spend one year training in the minor leagues, with the Montreal

31
00:04:00.562 --> 00:04:06.164
Royals, and then, if he did well, Robinson would join the major-league Dodgers. During that first year, Robinson encountered the obstacles

32
00:04:06.165 --> 00:04:10.842
Rickey had predicted. Some teams did not want to play against a black man and found excuses to cancel their

33
00:04:10.843 --> 00:04:17.379
games. When they did play, opposing teams and their fans ridiculed Robinson. In Syracuse, one player threw a black cat

34
00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:23.972
onto the field and shouted, "Hey, Jackie, there’s your cousin." Not being able to respond felt like a punishment. Robinson

35
00:04:23.973 --> 00:04:30.488
couldn’t eat. He couldn’t sleep. His hair started to turn gray. His doctor worried that Robinson might have a nervous

36
00:04:30.489 --> 00:04:39.964
breakdown. But Robinson fought back with his baseball skills. He made games exciting by batting well and by stealing bases

37
00:04:39.965 --> 00:04:47.083
to help his team score runs. He led the Royals to the league championship&#8212;often called "the pennant"&#8212;and later to the Junior

38
00:04:47.084 --> 00:04:53.454
World Series. When he joined the Brooklyn Dodgers in 1947, however, Robinson had to start all over. Some teammates circulated

39
00:04:53.455 --> 00:04:58.009
a petition demanding that he not join the team. He later wrote, "I had to fight hard against loneliness, abuse,

40
00:04:58.010 --> 00:05:08.885
and the knowledge that any mistake I made would be magnified because I was the only black man out there."

41
00:05:08.886 --> 00:05:14.676
But slowly, Robinson won the respect of his teammates and of much of the crowd. When opposing players tried to

42
00:05:14.677 --> 00:05:22.614
ridicule the popular Dodgers player Pee Wee Reese for playing with Robinson, Reese didn’t answer. Instead, he simply put a

43
00:05:22.615 --> 00:05:27.841
friendly arm around his teammate’s shoulder to show his support. Once united, the Dodgers became an outstanding team. Robinson was named

44
00:05:27.842 --> 00:05:35.603
Rookie of the Year in 1947. He won the Most Valuable Player award in 1949, as the National League batting

45
00:05:35.604 --> 00:05:43.540
leader with an average of .342. Robinson played with the Dodgers for ten years, helping them win six pennants. In

46
00:05:43.541 --> 00:05:48.697
1955, they won the World Series, and in 1962, Jackie Robinson became the first black man to be admitted into

47
00:05:48.698 --> 00:05:59.500
the Baseball Hall of Fame. Rickey and Robinson knew their "noble experiment" had succeeded when other teams in the major

48
00:05:59.501 --> 00:06:07.707
leagues started recruiting African Americans. Finally, Robinson could break his silence and stand up for himself against abuse. He had earned

49
00:06:07.708 --> 00:06:16.717
the right to speak his mind. He would later write, "I paid more than my dues for the right to

50
00:06:16.718 --> 00:06:22.564
call it like I see it." In 1956, Robinson recognized his playing days were nearing an end. His health and

51
00:06:22.565 --> 00:06:28.126
performance were suffering, and the Dodgers hoped to trade him to another team. Robinson didn’t want to go anywhere else,

52
00:06:28.127 --> 00:06:33.751
so he retired from baseball. He became vice president of personnel at Chock Full o’Nuts Corporation, overseeing the welfare of

53
00:06:33.752 --> 00:06:38.367
over one thousand people, mostly African Americans. He also continued to work for civil rights, raising money for the National

54
00:06:38.368 --> 00:06:47.702
Association for the Advancement of Colored People and combating racism alongside Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. In 1972, at a World

55
00:06:47.703 --> 00:06:58.747
Series game on the 25th anniversary of his Dodgers debut, Robinson told the crowd he wouldn’t be satisfied until more

56
00:06:58.748 --> 00:07:11.967
blacks worked in baseball’s upper management. He felt that although African Americans had come a long way in their fight

57
00:07:11.968 --> 00:07:21.556
for civil rights, obstacles still prevented them from reaching leadership positions. By then, Jackie Robinson was nearly blind from diabetes.

58
00:07:21.557 --> 00:07:32.012
He died of a heart attack not two weeks later, on October 24, 1972. Today Robinson’s number, 42, lives on

59
00:07:32.013 --> 00:07:45.383
in history. Out of respect for this extraordinary baseball player and champion of civil rights, it was retired&#8212;no new players

60
00:07:45.384 --> 00:07:52.208
can use it. Robinson once said, "A life is not important except in the impact it has on other lives." By

61
00:07:52.209 --> 00:08:00.583
breaking baseball’s color barrier in 1945, Jackie Robinson became the face of the civil-rights movement in the United States. Since

62
00:08:00.584 --> 00:08:09.878
then, many African Americans have achieved important successes in the battle for equal rights, but this baseball player scored first.

